#!/usr/bin/env perl
# PODNAME: robocop
# ABSTRACT: OCP robocop controller (in-cluster worker management)

use strict;
use warnings;
use FindBin;
use lib "$FindBin::Bin/../lib";

our $VERSION = '0.001';
use OCP::Robocop::Controller;

# The Deployment starts this as `robocop controller` (share/robocop/deployment.yaml).
# That is the only mode. The argument-less path used to run a CRIU-checkpoint
# stub that listened on port 9999 for a key nobody sent (k33, k2); in-memory
# key delivery is now part of the controller itself (security_level inject),
# so anything else is a usage error, reported on STDERR.
#
# run() returns only by dying (k170): the watch client cannot reach the API,
# at startup or for several checks in a row. That exits 1 with the reason on
# STDERR, so the pod restarts into CrashLoopBackOff instead of idling silently.
if (@ARGV && $ARGV[0] eq 'controller') {
    my $ok = eval {
        OCP::Robocop::Controller->from_env->run;
        1;
    };
    exit 0 if $ok;
    print STDERR 'robocop: fatal: ' . $@;
    exit 1;
}

print STDERR "usage: robocop controller\n"
    . "  Runs the OCP in-cluster controller; configured from the environment\n"
    . "  (see 'perldoc robocop').\n";
exit 2;

__END__

=head1 NAME

robocop - OCP Kubernetes controller for worker management

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    # Reconciliation controller (how the Deployment starts it)
    robocop controller

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Robocop is the in-cluster controller that manages worker nodes automatically.
This script is the entry point for the robocop Deployment in Kubernetes.

=over 4

=item B<controller>

Builds an L<OCP::Robocop::Controller> from the environment (via
C<< OCP::Robocop::Controller->from_env >>) and watches OCPNode custom resources,
reconciling each through L<OCP::Node>. This is the command the Deployment runs
(C<share/robocop/deployment.yaml>).

=back

Any other invocation prints the usage to STDERR and exits 2.

=head1 EXIT STATUS

B<controller> runs until it fails. A missing or invalid environment variable,
or a Kubernetes API that the watch client cannot reach -- at startup, or for
several checks in a row while running -- ends it with exit status 1 and the
reason on STDERR, so Kubernetes restarts the pod and shows the failure as
C<CrashLoopBackOff>.

=head1 ENVIRONMENT

The B<controller> subcommand reads its configuration from the environment:

=over 4

=item B<ROBOCOP_SECURITY_LEVEL> (optional)

C<inject> switches to in-memory key delivery: robocop starts without a key and
waits for C<ocp inject-key> (see L<OCP::Robocop::Controller/Key injection
(security_level inject)>). Any other value, or none, means the key comes from
C<ROBO_SSH_KEY>.

=item B<ROBO_SSH_KEY> (required, except with inject)

The robo-key private key material, used to reach freshly provisioned workers.
Must not be set with C<ROBOCOP_SECURITY_LEVEL=inject>.

=item B<ROBO_SSH_PUBLIC_KEY> (required with inject)

The robo key's public half. An injected key is accepted only if it matches.

=item B<RKE2_SERVER_URL> (required)

The RKE2/K3s server (join) URL, e.g. C<https://police1:9345>.

=item B<RKE2_TOKEN> (required)

The node join token.

=item B<NAMESPACE> (optional)

Namespace to watch; defaults to C<ocp-system>. Set from the pod's
C<metadata.namespace> in the Deployment.

=item B<OCP_DISTRIBUTION> (optional)

C<rke2> (default) or C<k3s>.

=item B<ROBOCOP_RESYNC_INTERVAL> (optional)

Seconds between two passes that enqueue every OCPNode still on its way to
Ready again; default C<60>.

=item B<ROBOCOP_MAX_RECONCILES> (optional)

How many OCPNodes are reconciled at the same time, each in its own process;
default C<2>.

=back

A missing required variable is fatal, with the offending name reported on
STDERR.

=head1 SEE ALSO

L<OCP::Robocop::Controller>, L<OCP::Robocop::KeyInjection>, L<OCP::Robocop>

=cut
